In linear regression with self-selection bias under the maximum selection criterion, a sample $(x,y_{\max}) \in \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}$ is generated as follows: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item A covariate $x$ is drawn from $\normal{0}{I}$, and 
    \item $y_{\max}$ is the maximum of $k$ different unknown linear functions of $x$ that are independently perturbed by noise, \emph{i.e.}{}, $y_{\max} = \max_{i \in [k]} \set{x^\top w_i^\star + \xi_i}$, where $w_1^\star,\dots,w_k^\star$ are the unknown target parameters and $\xi_1,\dots,\xi_k$ are independent $\normal{0}{1}$ random variables.
\end{enumerate}
The unknown parameters $w_1^\star,\dots,w_k^\star$ satisfy the following conditions: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(a)] (Separability)~~  $ \relax{w^\star_i}_2^2 
        \geq \Omega(1) + \max_{j\neq i}~\sabs{\sinangle{w^\star_j, w^\star_i}}.$
    \item[(b)] (Boundedness) ~~$\max_{1\leq i\leq k}\relax{w^\star_i}_2\leq O(1)\,.$
\end{enumerate}
\label{def:ssb}

\label{infthm:Intro1}
\label{infthm:Intro:SS}
Under \Cref{def:ssb}, there is an algorithm for linear regression with self-selection bias under the maximum selection criterion that recovers the weights $w^\star_1,w^\star_2,\dots,w^\star_k$ up to $\varepsilon$-error in $\mathrm{poly}(d,k,\nfrac{1}{\varepsilon}) + {k}^{O(k)}$ time using $O(d) \cdot \mathrm{poly}(k,\nfrac{1}{\varepsilon})$ samples.\footnote{{As in previous work, we recover the weights up to permutation, which is information-theoretically optimal.}}

\end{inftheorem} 
        Our approach to prove \cref{infthm:Intro:SS,infthm:Intro:SS:localConvergence},  
        is geometric, rooted in a reduction to a classical statistical problem known as \textit{coarsening} \cite{heitjan1990inference} and complements the important analytic techniques of prior works \cite{cherapanamjeri2023selfselection,gaitonde2024selfselection} 
        by offering a new perspective to the self-selection problem.

In the coarse learning problem, the  algorithm has access to an oracle that operates as follows:
\begin{center}
    {On each query, the oracle samples $x\sim \normal{\mu^\star}{I}$ and returns the unique set $P$ {in} $\hypo{P}$ containing $x.$}
\end{center}
We denote the distribution on sets $P\in \hypo{P}$ as $\coarseNormal{\mu^\star}{I}{\hypo{P}}.$
Crucially, the {actual} point $x$ itself is not observed.
{The challenge is to design an algorithm that, given i.i.d.{} (set) samples \(P_1, \dots, P_n \sim \coarseNormal{\mu^\star}{I}{\hypo{P}}\), accurately estimates \(\mu^\star\).}
{We focus on algorithms with time- and sample-complexity polynomial in the dimension and the desired accuracy, achieving an \(L_2\)-norm approximation of the true mean.

\label{def:informationPreservation}
            Given constants $\alpha\in [0,1]$, 
            true parameters $({\mu^\star},{\Sigma^\star})$ of a $d$-dimensional Gaussian distribution $\normal{{\mu^\star}}{{\Sigma^\star}}$, 
            and a partition $\hypo{P}$ of the domain $\mathbb{R}^d$, 
            $\hypo{P}$ is said to be \emph{$\alpha$-information preserving 
            at radius $R>0$
            with respect to $\normal{{\mu^\star}}{{\Sigma^\star}}$} if, 
            for any parameters $(\mu,\Sigma)$ 
            {that are $R$-close to $({\mu^\star},{\Sigma^\star})$ in $\ell_2$-norm},\footnote{
            For a matrix $A$, $\relax{A}_F$ denotes its Frobenious norm and is defined as $\relax{A}_F^2=\sum_i\sum_j A_{ij}^2$.}
            it holds that
            \[
                \tv{
                    \coarseNormal{\mu}{\Sigma}{\hypo{P}}
                }{  
                    \coarseNormal{{\mu^\star}}{{\Sigma^\star}}{\hypo{P}}
