\label{def:secondPrice}
In linear regression from second-price auction data, a sample $(x,i_{\max},y_{\max,2}) \in \mathbb{R}^d \times [k] \times \mathbb{R}$ is generated as follows: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $x$ is drawn from $\normal{0}{I}$,
    \item $i_{\max}$ is the index of the \emph{winner} when bids are generated by unknown linear functions of $x$ that are independently perturbed by noise, \emph{i.e.}{}, $i_{\max} = \operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}_{i \in [k]} x^\top w_i^\star + \xi_i$, where $w_1^\star,\dots,w_k^\star$ are the unknown target parameters and $\xi_1,\dots,\xi_k$ are independent $\normal{0}{1}$ random variables, and 
    \item ${y_{\max,2}}$ is the \emph{second-highest bid}, \emph{i.e.}{}, ${y_{\max,2}} = \max_{j \neq i} x^\top w_j^\star + \xi_j$.
\end{enumerate}
The unknown parameters $w_1^\star,\dots,w_k^\star$ satisfy the same conditions as in \cref{def:ssb}.

\label{rem:reliability}
Our results for second price auction data can be naturally extended to $k$-th price auctions. 
As mentioned by \cite{cherapanamjeri2022auctionEstimation}, {apart from their importance in auction theory,} these models are {also relevant for} reliability theory and machine autopsy -- which have received significant attention \cite{meilijson1981autopsy,antoine1993identifiability,nowik1990identifiability,gertsbakh1988statistical}; 
{while many of these works focus on the nonparametric setting,
they have natural parametric counterparts where our results are applicable}.

