    To show that $\ensuremath{\mathscr{L}}$ is strongly convex around $\inparen{{\mu^\star},{\Sigma^\star}}$, we compute its derivatives.

We can write the log-likelihood as follows
\[
    \ensuremath{\mathscr{L}}(\mu) = \sum_{P\in \hypo{P}} \normalMass{{\mu^\star}}{I}{P} \ensuremath{\mathscr{L}}_{P}(\mu)\,,
    \quadtext{where}
    \ensuremath{\mathscr{L}}_{P}(\mu)\coloneqq -\log\inparen{
        \normalMass{\mu}{I}{P}
    }\,.
    \addtocounter{equation}{1}\tag{\theequation}\label{eq:gradOfNLL:expansion}
\]
Due to the linearity of gradients, it suffices to compute $\grad\ensuremath{\mathscr{L}}_P(v, T)$ and $\grad^2\ensuremath{\mathscr{L}}_P(\mu)$ for each $P\in \hypo{P}$
to obtain the gradient and Hessian of $\ensuremath{\mathscr{L}}(\cdot)$.
Toward this, fix any $P\in \hypo{P}$.
Observe that 
\[
    \ensuremath{\mathscr{L}}_{P}(\mu) = 
    \log\frac{
        \int_{x\in \mathbb{R}^d} e^{
            -\frac{1}{2}(x-\mu)^\top(x-\mu)
        }{\rm d} x
    }{
        \int_{x\in P} e^{
            -\frac{1}{2}(x-\mu)^\top(x-\mu)
        }{\rm d} x
    }
    = 
    \log\frac{
        \int_{x\in \mathbb{R}^d}
        e^{
            -\frac12 \relax{x}_2^2 + x^\top \mu
        }{\rm d} x
    }{
        \int_{x\in P}
        e^{
            -\frac12 \relax{x}_2^2 + x^\top \mu
        }{\rm d} x
    }
    \,.
\]
Write $f(x; \mu) \coloneqq \exp(-\frac12 \relax{x}_2^2 + x^\top \mu)$. 
It follows that 
\begin{align*}
    \grad_\mu \ensuremath{\mathscr{L}}_P(\mu)
    &= \frac{\int_P f}{\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} f}\cdot \left[ \frac{(\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \grad f) (\int_P f) - (\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} f)(\int_P \grad f)}{(\int_P f)^2} \right] \\
    &= \frac{\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \grad f}{\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} f} - \frac{\int_P \grad f}{\int_P f}\,.
    \addtocounter{equation}{1}\tag{\theequation}\label{eq:gradOfNLL:compressed}
\end{align*}
Simplifying the expression and substituting the values of $f, \grad f$ gives
\[
    \grad \ensuremath{\mathscr{L}}_{P}(\mu) 
    = 
    \operatornamewithlimits{\mathbb{E}}_{\normal{\mu}{I}}\insquare{
            x
    }
    - 
    \operatornamewithlimits{\mathbb{E}}_{\truncatedNormal{P}{\mu}{I}}\insquare{
            x
    }\,.
\]
Substituting this in \cref{eq:gradOfNLL:expansion} gives the desired expression for $\grad \ensuremath{\mathscr{L}}(v,T)$.

To compute $\grad^2 \ensuremath{\mathscr{L}}_{P}(\mu)$,
